## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical implementation of PIM for cross-tenant vendor access scenarios; validates understanding of governance, auditability, and emergency access controls in real-world incident response environments.

## Key Concepts
- Privileged Identity Management (PIM)
- Eligible vs Active assignments
- Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)
- Approval workflows
- Just-in-time activation
- Conditional Access integration
- Audit logging and monitoring
- Automatic revocation mechanisms
- Cross-tenant access patterns
- Time-bound access controls
- Compliance reporting

## Code Example
```javascript
// PIM activation request configuration
const pimRequest = {
  roleId: 'incident-admin-role',
  justification: 'Critical incident response - vendor access required',
  duration: 'PT4H', // 4 hours maximum
  ticketInfo: {
    system: 'ServiceNow',
    ticketId: 'INC001234',
    ticketUrl: 'https://company.service-now.com/incident'
  },
  approvers: ['security-team@company.com'],
  mfaRequired: true,
  activationType: 'just-in-time'
};

// Monitor PIM activation logs
function monitorPIMActivations() {
  return {
    startTime: '2024-01-15T10:00:00Z',
    endTime: '2024-01-15T14:00:00Z',
    filters: {
      activity: 'Activate',
      targetResources: ['incident-portal-app'],
      initiatedBy: 'external-vendors'
    }
  };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates cross-tenant governance, certificate-based service principal authentication, and automated credential rotation at scale—essential competencies for enterprise SaaS integrations.

## Key Concepts
- Certificate rotation with Key Vault integration, including automatic expiration monitoring and secret versioning
- Least-privilege Graph permissions configured for multi-tenant automation scenarios
- Sandbox validation environments, comprehensive auditing across Azure AD and partner tenants
- Managed Identity usage for secure, credential-less certificate retrieval

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: fetch cert from Key Vault and prepare Graph app update
const { DefaultAzureCredential } = require('@azure/identity');
const { SecretClient } = require('@azure/keyvault-secrets');

async function getCertificate() {
  const credential = new DefaultAzureCredential();
  const client = new SecretClient('https://your-vault.vault.azure.net', credential);
  const certificate = await client.getSecret('partner-sp-certificate');
  return certificate.value;
}
```
